      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment (part 2 of 2) of an OIML certificate.\n\nKey content:\n- OIML Certificate No: R76/1992-GB1-10.04, Revision 3\n- Construction details\n- Load cell: CAS load cell, model SW → component\n- Devices list\n- Technical data: mains adaptor 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1 and ER JR Models) or 9 V DC (PW-II Model); battery options per model; temperature range -10 to +40 °C\n- Sealing\n- Alternative manufacturer: CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd, address\n- Certificate History table → revision_history\n\nModel variants mentioned: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II, plus earlier mentioned SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED Models.\n\nActually the devices section says \"Check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED Models only)\". So the model family includes: SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II, ED, ER PLUS, SW-1.\n\nThe certificate is R76/1992-GB1-10.04 — this is a non-automatic weighing instrument certificate. Scheme: R76 is the recommendation. The scheme field under certificate: \"A\" or \"B\"? OIML-CS certificates... R76/1992-GB1-10.04 — issued by GB1 (UK, NWML?). Actually the format \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" suggests country GB, number 1. The scheme (A|B) — not explicitly stated in fragment. Since not present, omit.\n\nSince this is a fragment, I should only extract what's present:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\"\n- revision? The schema doesn't have revision at cert level except revision_history. The \"Revision 3\" appears. I could put... schema doesn't have a field. Maybe omit, revision_history captures it.\n\nissuing_authority: not present in this fragment (no name/address given). Omit.\n\napplicants: not present in fragment (only alternative manufacturer). Omit.\n\nmanufacturers[]:\n- Alternative manufacturer: CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd, 99# Changjiang Road, Jiashan County, Zhejiang Province, China. Since the heading says \"Alternative manufacturer\", include as a manufacturer entry. Hmm, is \"Alternative manufacturer\" a manufacturer? Yes — record it in manufacturers[].\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — R76 is for non-automatic weighing instruments. The fragment doesn't explicitly state category... but \"Load cell is a CAS load cell, model SW\" and weighing instrument. The schema allows category. I could infer \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" from R76. Rule 8 says don't fabricate. But category is reasonable inference from R76 recommendation. Hmm, risky. The fragment mentions \"instruments\", load receptor, Max, tare — clearly NAWS. I'll include category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Actually, to be safe, maybe include since R76 is definitively NAWS.\n\n- type_designations: The models: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR. Also \"model SW\" load cell is a component designation. The certified type designations would be the model names. Include: [\"ED\", \"ER PLUS\", \"SW-1\", \"ER JR\", \"PW-II\", \"SW-1S\", \"SW-1C\", \"SW-1W\", \"SW-1LR\", \"SW-1WR\"]. Hmm, but SW-1 covers SW-1S etc.? \"SW-1 Model\" listed separately from SW-1S... In the battery section: \"(SW-1 Model)\". In modes: \"(SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED Models only)\". So SW-1S etc. are variants of SW-1. I'll list all explicitly mentioned: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR.\n\n- module_designation: null / omit.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: maybe \"SW\" — the load cell is model SW, but instruments? The certificate title likely relates to CAS SW/ER/PW series. Not explicit. family_name: null or \"SW\"? The load cell model is SW. Hmm. The certified instruments: SW-1S, ER PLUS etc. Not a single explicit family name. I'll use null... Actually maybe omit family_name or set null. Schema says include family_name explicitly if any else null.\n\n- models[]: entries per