lue\": 6}, {\"model\": \"ER PLUS\", \"value\": 6}, {\"model\": \"ER JR\", \"value\": 6}, {\"model\": \"SW-1\", \"value\": 6}, {\"model\": \"PW-II\", \"value\": 9}]}\n  \n  PW-II: 6 x 1.5 V = 9 V. That's computed — is that fabrication? 6×1.5=9, straightforward. Hmm, rule 8: don't fabricate. Computing 9 V from 6 x 1.5 V is derivation, borderline. Alternatively record \"battery_capacity\" for rechargeable: 3.6 Ah (ED, ER PLUS, ER JR), 3.5 Ah (SW-1). And battery configuration strings.\n\n  Simpler: record as model_level attributes:\n  - \"power_supply_voltage\": {values: ED 12, ER PLUS 12, SW-1 12, ER JR 12, PW-II 9, unit V}\n  - \"battery_type\": string values per model.\n\nDevices section — could be recorded as characteristics config? e.g., initial_zero_setting_device: ≤ 20% of Max; semi_automatic_zero_setting_device ≤ 4% Max; zero_tracking ≤ 4% Max; tare... These are device features with values. Schema characteristics.type_level could include:\n- \"initial_zero_setting_range\": {max: 20, unit: \"% of Max\"} → value {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol \"% of Max\"? The structured form: value {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol \"%Max\". Hmm.\n- \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_range\": {max: 4} \"% of Max\"\n- \"zero_tracking_range\": {max: 4} \"% of Max\"\n\nTare: \"T ≤ -50% Max (single-interval)\" and \"T ≤ -Max₁ (dual-interval)\" — config_level axis \"interval_count\": single vs dual. Values: condition \"single-interval\", value -50 %Max; condition \"dual-interval\", value \"-Max₁\" (string with subscript... avoid LaTeX; \"Max1\"?). Rule 1 says avoid footnote markers and LaTeX. \"Max₁\" is a unicode subscript. I could keep as \"Max1\" or \"-Max1\". Value: \"-Max1\", unit null.\n\nMaybe I'm overcomplicating. Devices could be listed as components? No — devices are features. I could include a type_level characteristic set for the zero-setting devices since values given.\n\nI'll include:\n- type_level:\n  - initial_zero_setting_device_max: {value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"% of Max\"} — hmm unit \"%Max\". Canonical: \"initial_zero_setting_range\". Let me use attribute \"initial_zero_setting_device_max_range\"? Keep simpler: \"initial_zero_setting_range\".\n  \n  Actually let me not over-engineer; include:\n  - temperature_range: {min:-10, max:40} °C\n  - initial_zero_setting_range: {max: 20} \"%\" (of Max) — value {min:null, max:20}, unit \"% of Max\"? unit_symbol should be a unit; \"% of Max\" is not pure unit. I'll use unit_symbol \"%\", and the \"of Max\" is implied by attribute naming? Could add reference \"Max\". Eh. I'll set unit_symbol \"%\" and attribute names make it clear (e.g., \"initial_zero_setting_limit\"). Hmm, the value \"≤ 20% of Max\" — reference is Max. I'll do:\n    {\"initial_zero_setting_device\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"}} — unit_id null. It's fine; unit_symbol is a raw symbol string.\n  \n  Actually rule says \"Pure units or footnote markers are NOT values\" — \"% of Max\" is a proportion reference, fine.\n\n- config_level:\n  - {\"attribute\": \"tare_range\", \"axis\": \"instrument_interval_type\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": -50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"}, {\"condition\": \"dual-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"-Max1\", \"unit_symbol\": null}]}\n\nHmm \"T ≤ - 50 % Max\" — tare is subtractive, so range down to -50% of Max. value {min: null, max: -50}. OK.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- power_supply_voltage: values per model as above.\n- mains_adaptor: \"any compatible CE-marked\" — type_level? \"mains_adaptor_requirement\": \"any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor\". Could be a description. Skip or include as type_level string. I'll include as type_level: {\"mains_adaptor\": {\"value\": \"any compatible CE-marked\"}}. Maybe skip; not essential. I'll include it as a descriptive type_level characteristic — acceptable since value can be string.\n\n- battery: model_level string values:\n  - ED: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"\n  - ER PLUS: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"\n  - ER JR: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah or 4 x 1.5 V D type batteries\"\n  - SW-1: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah\"\n  - PW-II: \"integrated 6 x 1.5 V AA batteries\"\n  - SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR: not specified (they may fall under SW-1 model but unclear) — omit them.\n\nBattery voltage/capacity numeric: could add \"battery_capacity\": ED/ER PLUS/ER JR 3.6 Ah, SW-1 3.5 Ah. That's clean numeric. And \"battery_voltage\": 6 V for ED/ER PLUS/ER JR/SW-1; PW-II: 6x1.5=9 V → compute? I'd rather include battery_configuration strings and battery_capacity numeric. Let me do:\n\nmodel_level:\n1. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [{model ED, 12}, {ER PLUS, 12}, {SW-1, 12}, {ER JR, 12}, {PW-II, 9}]}\n2. {\"attribute\": \"battery_configuration\", \"values\": [{model, string}...]} — value strings, unit null.\n3. {\"attribute\": \"battery_capacity\", \"unit_symbol\": \"Ah\", \"values\": [{ED, 3.6}, {ER PLUS, 3.6}, {ER JR, 3.6}, {SW-1, 3.5}]} — omit PW-II (non-rechargeable, no Ah given).\n\nHmm, battery_configuration and battery_capacity overlap. Keep both? The rules prefer structured. I'll include both but maybe just battery_configuration to be safe plus capacity. Fine.\n\nAlso power_supply_type: DC — applies to all. type_level: {\"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"DC\"}}.\n\nmatrix_tables: none in this fragment (certificate history is revision_history, not a matrix).\n\ntest_reports: none.\n\nrecommendation: R76, edition 1992? \"R76/1992\" — edition year 1992. The canonical id \"R76\", edition: 1992? The schema says edition (int). R76/1992 — edition could be 1992 (year-based edition) or the edition number... OIML R76-1 1992 edition. I'd put edition: 1992. Hmm — is that fabrication? The cert number contains R76/1992, so edition 1992 is directly derivable. accuracy_classes: not stated in fragment — omit.\n\nBut wait: this is a fragment; part 1 probably had recommendation details. The instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment only. The cert number \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" is present. Including recommendation {id: \"R76\", edition: 1992} is derivable from the number. I think it's fine to include; but cautious — rules say don't fabricate fields not present. The recommendation identifier is present in the cert number. I'll include recommendation: {\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 1992}. accuracy_classes omitted.\n\nrevision_history:\n- revision \"0\"? First issued 27 May 2009 → revision... the initial issue has no rev number. revision: \"0\"? Schema example uses \"0\". The history: \n  - R76/1992-GB1-10.04, 27 May 2009, \"Certificate first issued\" → revision \"0\"\n  - rev 1, 04 September 2012, \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"\n  - rev 2, 12 May 2014, \"Single-interval instruments added.\\nMax tare value added to devices section.\\nSealing section added.\"\n  - rev 3, 19 May 2014, \"Applicant's address changed from: 19 Ganap-Ri\\nGwangjuk-Myoun\\nYangju-Si\\nGyeonggi-Do 482-841\\nRepublic of Korea\\nAlternative manufacturer added.\"\n\nInclude those with changes as strings.\n\nfootnotes: none.\n\nissuing_authority: not in fragment — omit.\n\ncertificate fields: number \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\". scheme — OIML-CS scheme A/B; cert format 1992 era predates CS; not stated. Omit. page_total: 4 (\"This certificate includes 4 pages\") — derivable! Include page_total: 4. project_number: not present. member_state: not explicit (GB1 hints UK but not stated; don't fabricate). date_issued: revision 3 date 19 May 2014? The cert current revision issued 19 May 2014 — that's the last revision date. Could set date_issued \"2014-05-19\". Hmm — it's the revision date, arguably the issue date of current version. I'll include date_issued: \"2014-05-19\". Or omit to be safe? The revision 3 entry gives date. I think including date_issued as 2014-05-19 is reasonable. I'll include.\n\ncertified_type.description: Could summarize construction? Construction: Steel construction, operator's keypad, stainless steel load receptor, level indicator, operator and customer display. I could put in description. Also sealing. Let me include a description string combining construction features. That's faithful.\n\nComponents:\n- load cell: role \"load_cell\", type_designations [\"SW\"], maybe characteristics {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\"}? characteristics{} shape isn't strictly defined for components. I'll add characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": {\"value\": \"CAS\", ...}}? Simpler: put type_designations [\"CAS SW\"]? The text: \"CAS load cell, model SW\". I'll do type_designations: [\"SW\"], and characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\"} — but schema says characteristics{} without specifying; free-form ok. To follow structured-value strictness, maybe characteristics: {} omitted. I'll include \"manufacturer\" note via... hmm. components entries have role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll set characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": {\"value\": \"CAS\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}}? That seems overly rigid. The strict structured-value rule applies to characteristic values. I'll include it consistently.\n\nActually simpler: type_designations: [\"CAS SW\"]. Hmm that merges maker+model. The rule for load cells in R60 examples uses model only. I'll do type_designations [\"SW\"] and characteristics {\"manufacturer\": ...}. Fine.\n\nmodels list — which models? Explicitly named: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II. Also \"model SW\" refers to load cell, not instrument. Include the 10 instrument models.\n\nFor model_level attributes, I need model entries matching. For power_supply_voltage: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR get 12; PW-II gets 9. The SW-1S... variants unspecified — omit.\n\nNote: \"12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1 and ER JR Models)\". So models: ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR.\n\nBattery:\n- \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah battery (ED, ER PLUS and ER JR Models)\"\n- \"integrated 4 x 1.5 V D type batteries (ER JR Model)\"\n- \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah battery (SW-1 Model)\"\n- \"integrated 6 x 1.5 V AA batteries (PW-II Model)\"\n\nSo ER JR has two options (OR). battery_configuration values:\n- ED: \"rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"\n- ER PLUS: \"rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"\n- ER JR: \"rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah OR 4 x 1.5 V D type\"\n- SW-1: \"rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah\"\n- PW-II: \"6 x 1.5 V AA\"\n\nOK.\n\nDevices as type_level characteristics — decide final list:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {max 20} \"% of Max\"\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {max 4} \"% of Max\"\n- zero_tracking_range: {max 4} \"% of Max\"\n\nHmm \"≤ 20% of Max\" means the device range is up to 20% of Max. Value {min: null, max: 20}.\n\nAlternatively name them with \"_limit\". I'll go with these attribute names.\n\nconfig_level tare:\nattribute \"tare_range\", axis \"instrument type\", values:\n- {condition: \"single-interval instruments\", value: {min:null, max:-50}, unit_symbol: \"% of Max\"}\n- {condition: \"dual-interval instruments\", value: \"-Max1\", unit_symbol: null}\n\nWait \"T ≤ - 50 % Max\" — T is the tare, negative, magnitude ≤50% Max. Represent value {min: null, max: -50}. OK.\n\nMax₁ → write \"Max1\"? Avoid subscript char? Unicode subscript ₁ is fine in JSON but rule 1 says no LaTeX/footnote markers; subscript char isn't LaTeX. I'll use \"Max1\" to be safe. Value string \"-Max1\".\n\nSealing: description — include in certified_type.description or a characteristic \"sealing_method\": \"wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker\". I'll add to description text.\n\nModes: \"Check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED Models only)\" — this is a model-dependent feature. Could be config/model_level: attribute \"check_weighing_and_unstable_sample_modes\", values per model boolean true for those models. Eh — could add model_level {\"attribute\": \"check_weighing_and_weighing_unstable_samples_modes\", \"values\": [...models true...]}. I'll include as a model_level attribute with value true. Or fold into description. I'll include as model_level for completeness — models: SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II, ED → true.\n\nConstruction items could be description.\n\nGravity compensation, zero indicator, net indicator — features; skip detailed extraction, put in description maybe. I'll write a description sentence listing construction and devices.\n\nActually description: \"Steel construction; operator's keypad; stainless steel load receptor; level indicator; operator and customer display. Zero, tare and gravity compensation devices; sealing by wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker.\" Something concise.\n\nLet me assemble JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\",\n    \"page_total\": 4,\n    \"date_issued\": \"2014-05-19\"\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R76\",\n    \"edition\": 1992\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"99# Changjiang Road\", \"Jiashan County\", \"Zhejiang Province\", \"China\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ED\", \"ER PLUS\", \"SW-1\", \"SW-1S\", \"SW-1C\", \"SW-1W\", \"SW-1LR\", \"SW-1WR\", \"ER JR\", \"PW-II\"],\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [{\"model_id\": \"ED\"}, {\"model_id\": \"ER PLUS\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1S\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1C\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1W\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1LR\"}, {\"model_id\": \"SW-1WR\"}, {\"model_id\": \"ER JR\"}, {\"model_id\": \"PW-II\"}]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"load_cell\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"SW\"],\n      \"characteristics\": {\"manufacturer\": {\"value\": \"CAS\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"DC\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"mains_adaptor\": {\"value\": \"any compatible CE-marked\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"initial_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"zero_tracking_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"ED\", \"value\": 12, ...}, ...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"battery_configuration\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"battery_capacity\", \"unit_symbol\": \"Ah\", \"values\": [{\"ED\",3.6},{\"ER PLUS\",3.6},{\"ER JR\",3.6},{\"SW-1\",3.5}]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"check_weighing_and_unstable_samples_modes\", \"values\": [true for the 8 models]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"subtractive_tare_range\", \"axis\": \"instrument type\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": -50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"}, {\"condition\": \"dual-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"-Max1\", \"unit_symbol\": null}]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2009-05-27\", \"changes\": \"Certificate first issued\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-09-04\", \"changes\": \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2014-05-12\", \"changes\": \"Single-interval instruments added. Max tare value added to devices section. Sealing section added.\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2014-05-19\", \"changes\": \"Applicant's address changed from 19 Ganap-Ri, Gwangjuk-Myoun, Yangju-Si, Gyeonggi-Do 482-841, Republic of Korea. Alternative manufacturer added.\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nWait — revision \"0\": the first issue had no revision label; the row says ISSUE NO. \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" with date. Use revision \"0\". Acceptable per schema example (\"0\").\n\nThe value objects in model_level — shape: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. Per R60 example, values entries just {model, value}. Include footnote_markers only if needed. I'll keep {model, value} minimal, maybe include footnote_markers: [] — not necessary. The structured-value strictness (value/unit objects) applies to characteristics values; model_level entries use {model, value} per examples, with unit at attribute level. Follow examples.\n\nFor battery_configuration values with strings — value strings fine.\n\nAlso \"gravity compensation\" — feature, in description.\n\ndescription: \"Steel construction with operator's keypad, stainless steel load receptor, level indicator, and operator and customer display. Equipped with initial zero setting, semi-automatic zero setting, zero tracking, zero indicator, net indicator, semi-automatic subtractive tare balancing device and gravity compensation. Sealing by wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker prevents access to the load cell and calibration switch.\"\n\ncertified_type.category — I'm fairly confident: R76 = NAWS. Include.\n\nAlso should certificate include \"scheme\"? Not present; omit.\n\nmember_state: not present; omit.\n\nOne more check: \"Revision 3\" appears at top — certificate current revision; revision_history covers it.\n\nDate formats: use ISO? Schema doesn't specify; I'll use ISO YYYY-MM-DD.\n\nShould I include \"date_issued\": \"2014-05-19\"? The revision 3 date is when the current version issued. I'll include it — reasonable. Or omit to avoid fabricating? It's directly the last revision date = date of the certificate document at revision 3. Include.\n\npage_total: 4 — from \"This certificate includes 4 pages\". Include.\n\nAlso \"oiml_issuer_id\": not present.\n\nDouble-check devices values: \"Initial zero setting device (≤ 20% of Max)\" → max 20 % of Max. \"Semi-automatic zero setting device (≤ 4% of Max)\" → 4. \"Zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max)\" → 4. Good.\n\nTare: \"T ≤ - 50 % Max (single-interval instruments)\". I'll normalize to condition \"single-interval instruments\" value {min: null, max: -50} unit \"% of Max\". \"T ≤ - Max₁ (dual-interval instruments)\" → value \"-Max1\" unit null.\n\nFinal JSON, no markdown fences. Ensure no trailing commas.",
